**Call type:** Market Volatility & Panic Reassurance - **Length:** 08:45

**Client persona:** Retail Investor (IT Professional, 36 yrs old, ₹35,000 monthly SIP running for 2.5 years, anxious about recent negative 1-year returns)

## Transcript
**Advisor:** Good afternoon, Mr. Rajesh! Thank you for scheduling this call with FundsIndia. I noticed you requested a review of your Wealth Builder portfolio today. How have things been with you?

**Client:** Hi Amit. Honestly, I am quite stressed. I opened my FundsIndia app yesterday and saw my portfolio in red over the last 12 months. My midcap fund is down 9%. My colleagues are saying markets will fall another 15%, and I am seriously thinking of stopping my SIPs and redeeming before I lose more of my hard-earned money.

> **Key moment:** Empathy checkpoint: Do NOT interrupt or counter with statistics immediately. Validate the emotional experience first.


**Advisor:** I completely understand why seeing your portfolio in red feels uncomfortable, Rajesh. When you work hard for your monthly savings, watching the dashboard dip naturally triggers anxiety. I would feel the exact same impulse if I looked at a single year in isolation. Can we step back together and look at your primary goal for this portfolio?

**Client:** Well, this is my daughter Ananya''s higher education fund. She is currently 6 years old, so this money is needed when she turns 18 for her university admission.

**Advisor:** That gives us a clear 12-year runway. Rajesh, in the history of Indian equity markets over the last 25 years, every single rolling 7-to-10 year period has delivered positive compounding, even when investors lived through the 2008 Lehman crisis and the 2020 pandemic crash. When the market drops 10%, your ₹35,000 SIP is actually buying more mutual fund units at discounted NAVs—like a seasonal discount sale on quality assets.

> **Key moment:** Reframing technique: Connect current volatility to buying units at a discount for an undisrupted 12-year child goal.


**Client:** So stopping my SIP right now would actually lock in the loss and stop me from buying cheaper units?

**Advisor:** Precisely! Stopping your SIP pauses your rupee-cost averaging at the worst possible time. If anything, historical data shows that investors who maintained or stepped up their SIP during market pullbacks achieved 3% to 4% higher terminal returns by year 10. Let''s keep your existing asset allocation steady. Would you like me to set a quarterly review so we monitor your goal milestones together?

**Client:** That gives me immense clarity and peace of mind, Amit. Let''s continue the SIP without interruption. Please schedule the next check-in in 3 months.

